---
/**
 * Hampstead, the merged area page, 9 November 2026. Built hidden.
 *
 * WHY MERGED. Two versions went to Kaspar: this page on 6 Nov and a design-led
 * copy at /carpenter-hampstead-design/ on 7 Nov. Five independent reviews and
 * three skeptics found this one the better base for search (ops repo PR #32,
 * deliverables/area-pages/carpenter-hampstead-v1-v2-merge-plan.md), and a
 * founder asked on 9 Nov for the merged page: Step 1 (this page plus Kaspar's
 * 8 Nov answers) and Step 2 (a short Hampstead design section, which his own
 * heading and note for the design-led copy asked for).
 *
 * WHOSE WORDS.
 *   - Kaspar's, word for word: the four sections he rewrote on 8 Nov (period
 *     houses, how the work runs, conservation areas and listed buildings,
 *     parking and access), headings included, and the design section's heading
 *     and first paragraph. Tone protocol v1.4 section 1: the client wins. Where
 *     his words break one of the protocol's hard bans (a 9 Nov check found six,
 *     in all four sections), they go back to him rather than being reworded:
 *     the preview's question cards ask him to keep or drop them (F2F repo,
 *     clients/structure-and-style/hampstead-preview/questions.json). What he
 *     keeps is logged as a section 16 exception; what he drops comes out here.
 *     Nothing of his is reworded here.
 *   - The approved first-version copy, unchanged: the lede, the cost section,
 *     the guarantee and the four questions.
 *   - New: the design section's body, three parking lines after his (Hampstead's
 *     CA-H zone, Camden's zone and trade-permit pages) and one line on the
 *     process page at the end of the cost section. Written by a copy author who
 *     is neither the planner nor the builder (three drafts, one judged and
 *     fixed, then split to the protocol's 25-word limit after review, 9 Nov).
 *     All of it is held by areaHampsteadMerge until Kaspar approves it.
 *
 * THE GATE. areaHampstead1 (which Hampstead jobs he has done) holds the lede's
 * Hampstead claim, and areaHampsteadMerge holds the new copy. Until both are
 * answered the page is noindexed, out of the sitemap, missing from the footer's
 * Areas group and linked from nowhere. Keys 2 to 4 no longer hold this page:
 * his rewrites replaced the sentences they held. The design-led copy, kept
 * until his comments on it are dealt with, still uses them.
 *
 * Street and building names in the new copy are Camden's, quoted from its 2001
 * conservation area statement and attributed to it (brief fact rows 86 and
 * 106), not places we claim to have worked. The three renders are the journal
 * guides' files with their approved alt text, each captioned "AI-generated
 * example", which Kaspar's note at the top of the section explains.
 */
import AreaPage from "../components/AreaPage.astro";
import Held from "../components/Held.astro";
import Wrap from "../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Reviews from "../components/ds/Reviews.astro";
import Faq from "../components/ds/Faq.astro";
import Link from "../components/ds/Link.astro";
import Render from "../components/journal/Render.astro";
import Renders from "../components/journal/Renders.astro";

import { AREAS } from "../data/areas";
import { QUOTE_BANK, REVIEWS } from "../data/site-content";
import { ANSWERS, answered } from "../data/answers";

const area = AREAS.find((a) => a.slug === "carpenter-hampstead")!;

/* The reviews: every bank entry tagged with this district, in bank order,
   whole. A gated entry takes its byline from its key once answered. */
const quotes = QUOTE_BANK.filter((q) => q.areas.includes(area.name)).map((q) => ({
  text: q.text,
  meta: q.gate && answered(q.gate) ? String(ANSWERS[q.gate].value) : q.meta,
}));

const LIST = "list-style:none;margin:var(--space-10) 0 0;padding:0;border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)";
const ITEM = "border-top:var(--rule-hair);padding:var(--space-6) 0";
const P2 = "margin-top:var(--space-10)";
const TEXT = "max-width:var(--measure)";
const SECTION = "padding-bottom:var(--section-y)";
---

<AreaPage area={area}>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      {/* Not held since 9 Nov 2026: a founder ruled not to ask Kaspar about Hampstead jobs.
          This is his approved sentence, live on every service page. */}
      We design and fit <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">wardrobes</Link> across London, with much of our work in Hampstead, Belsize Park, Kentish Town, Camden and surrounding areas of north London.
    </p>
    <p style={P2}>
      We also fit <Link href="/walk-in-wardrobes/">walk-in wardrobes</Link>, <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link>, <Link href="/media-walls/">built-in TV units</Link> and <Link href="/bespoke-kitchens/">bespoke kitchens</Link>, each measured in the room and cut to follow its walls.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  {/* Kaspar's words, 8 Nov 2026 (his answer on the areaHampstead1 card). */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Fit" level={4}>Can bespoke fitted furniture be installed in Hampstead period homes and mansion flats?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Absolutely. Bespoke fitted furniture is particularly well suited to period properties, where walls, floors and ceilings are not always perfectly level or square. Each installation is carefully measured and designed to make the most of the available space while complementing the character of the property.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Hampstead has a wide variety of architectural styles, from Georgian houses on Church Row and Regency villas on Keats Grove to Victorian terraces and traditional mansion blocks. These properties often feature original architectural details, unusual alcoves and varying ceiling heights, making made-to-measure furniture a practical choice.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        We design and install bespoke wardrobes, alcove units, shelving and other fitted furniture in both period houses and mansion flats, taking care to achieve a neat, well-integrated finish.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Heading and first paragraph: Kaspar's (8 Nov, on the design-led copy). The
      rest: new copy, held until he approves it. */}
  <Wrap as="section" id="design" style={SECTION}>
    {/* "Design" is the only builder-chosen word on the page: every other section
        carries a short label above its heading, and this one needs one to match. */}
    <SectionHead meta="Design" level={4}>What fitted furniture works best in different Hampstead properties</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Images labelled "AI-generated example" are provided for design inspiration and to illustrate different fitted furniture options. All other project photographs on this page show our completed work.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        <Held k="areaHampsteadMerge"><Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/hampstead-conservation-area" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Camden's conservation area statement for Hampstead</Link> describes early 18th century houses in Church Row and Regency villas on Keats Grove. It also describes Victorian terraces off Willoughby Road, many with semi-basements, and mansion blocks such as Northcote Mansions.</Held>
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        <Held k="areaHampsteadMerge">In a Church Row or Keats Grove house, the choice is how the furniture meets the room. Units can stop below the cornice and leave it visible, or a cornice can run across the top. If the house is listed, consent comes first, before any change to the cornice or panelling.</Held>
      </p>
    </div>
    <Renders>
      <Render src="/images/journal/alcove-ai-below-cornice.webp" width={1536} height={1024} caption="AI-generated example" short="Stopping below the cornice" pair alt="Built-in alcove units combining warm lit display shelving with traditional framed cupboard doors and small brass knobs. The framed shelving stops beneath the room's cornice, leaving the original moulding and a band of wall visible above the units." />
      <Render src="/images/journal/alcove-ai-cornice-across.webp" width={1536} height={1024} caption="AI-generated example" short="Cornice across the top" pair alt="Traditional bespoke alcove units with framed shelving and panelled cupboards beneath, finished with a cornice that runs across the top of the units and around the chimney breast. Warm lighting highlights the display shelves, while the joinery complements the period fireplace." />
    </Renders>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p style={P2}>
        <Held k="areaHampsteadMerge">Camden also notes that many of the Willoughby Road houses have steps up to the front door. For a terrace like that, the route in matters as much as the room. Furniture that arrives in separate sections is easier to carry up the steps and through the hall than one large piece.</Held>
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        <Held k="areaHampsteadMerge">In a mansion block such as Northcote Mansions, a full wall of handleless flat doors keeps a bedroom plain and the floor clear.</Held>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <Render src="/images/journal/built-in-wardrobes-ai-handleless.webp" width={1536} height={1024} caption="AI-generated example" short="Handleless flat doors" alt="A wall-to-wall wardrobe with six full-height flat doors in a smooth sage finish, with no visible handles and thin, even gaps between them. A slim top infill meets the flat ceiling, slim side infills run down to the floor, and one continuous recessed plinth gives the base a shadow line." />
    </div>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p style={P2}>
        <Held k="areaHampsteadMerge">For layouts, shelf spacing and materials, see our <Link href="/journal/alcove-units-guide/">alcove units guide</Link> and <Link href="/journal/built-in-wardrobe-ideas/">built-in wardrobe ideas</Link>.</Held>
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Kaspar's words, 8 Nov 2026 (his answer on the areaHampstead3 card). */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="In your building" level={4}>How do installations work in period houses and managed apartment buildings?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Working in period properties and managed apartment buildings can require additional planning, particularly around access, deliveries, working hours and protecting the property.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        We normally work between 8am and 5pm on weekdays, although working hours can be adjusted to accommodate the client's preferences and any building requirements. Where necessary, we can coordinate with the building's managing agent and provide relevant insurance and safety documentation before installation begins.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        During installation, we take care to protect floors and surrounding areas, minimise dust and disruption, and keep the workspace clean and organised. When cutting materials indoors, we always use dust extraction equipment. We also make suitable arrangements for deliveries and access depending on the property.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Kaspar's words, 8 Nov 2026 (his answer on the areaHampstead2 card). */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Listing and consent" level={4}>Do conservation areas and listed buildings affect fitted furniture?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Many properties in Hampstead are located within conservation areas, including those around Church Row, Keats Grove and Downshire Hill. Being in a conservation area does not normally prevent homeowners from installing bespoke fitted furniture. Internal installations, such as fitted wardrobes, alcove units and shelving, generally do not require planning permission simply because a property is in a conservation area.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Listed buildings have additional protections, including their interiors. If an installation affects the building's special architectural or historic character, listed building consent may be required. This can include alterations to original features such as historic panelling, fireplaces or fitted cupboards.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        If your property is listed, we recommend checking with Camden Council before work begins to establish whether any permissions are necessary. We take care to respect existing architectural features and design fitted furniture to complement the character of the property.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        For further information, visit <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/listed-buildings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Camden Council's listed buildings guidance</Link>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Kaspar's words, 8 Nov 2026 (his answer on the areaHampstead4 card), then one
      new line, held until he approves it. */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Access" level={4}>How do parking and access work for installations in Hampstead?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        We discuss parking, deliveries and access arrangements with the client before installation begins. This includes identifying suitable loading and unloading areas and checking whether there are any access restrictions.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Where necessary, we may ask the client to arrange parking permits or provide information about building access, lifts and any restrictions on deliveries. This helps us plan the installation efficiently and minimise disruption.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        <Held k="areaHampsteadMerge">Camden's parking zone for Hampstead is CA-H, and nearby streets fall in other zones with their own days and hours. Camden's <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/controlled-parking-zones" target="_blank" rel="noopener">controlled parking zones page</Link> lets you search a street or postcode to see its zone and hours. For builders and other trades, Camden's <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/trade-permits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trade and other parking permits page</Link> says a permit does not guarantee a space.</Held>
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guide prices" level={4}>What does fitted furniture cost?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>Every figure below is a starting price, and a larger or more detailed project costs more.</p>
      <ul style={LIST}>
        <li style={ITEM}><Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">Fitted wardrobes</Link> start from £2,300 for a 2-door run up to 1.2m in melamine, and from £4,000 at 4 doors. Width, finish, internal drawers, integrated lighting and more complex designs move the figure.</li>
        <li style={ITEM}><Link href="/alcove-units/">Alcove units</Link> start from £3,300 for a pair in melamine and from £6,000 in oak veneer. Finish, shelving detail, drawers and lighting move the figure. It assumes base cupboards with shelving over, up to about 1m per alcove.</li>
        <li style={ITEM}><Link href="/walk-in-wardrobes/">Walk-in wardrobes</Link> typically start from around £5,000. The size of the room, number of fitted runs, internal layout, materials and finishes, and integrated lighting move the figure.</li>
        <li style={ITEM}><Link href="/media-walls/">Built-in TV units</Link> start from £3,500. Overall size, cabinetry and storage, integrated lighting, cable management and specialist finishes move the figure.</li>
        <li style={ITEM}><Link href="/bespoke-kitchens/">Bespoke kitchens</Link> typically start from £10,000. Kitchen size and layout, cabinetry and internal storage, worktops, appliances, and materials and finishes move the figure.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style={P2}>
        These are guide starting prices, and a fixed, itemised quotation follows the measured survey. Our <Link href="/fitted-wardrobe-cost/">cost guide</Link> sets out every step for fitted wardrobes and alcove units by size and finish.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        {/* Arthur's comment, 7 Nov 2026 ("u can see the elevations to get an idea how the unit would look"), added
            9 Nov. Kaspar's own answers back it: q20 (scaled drawings and elevations of the agreed design), q13 and
            q18 (only where a project needs design), stage04 (made to the approved drawings). Held for his yes. */}
        <Held k="areaHampsteadMerge">Where a project needs design, you get scaled drawings and elevations before anything is made, so you can see how it will look. The page on <Link href="/process/">our process</Link> covers four stages: survey and planning, quotation and design, preparation, and fitting.</Held>
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  {/* "From N Google reviews" and the link to all of them are the component's, from REVIEWS.count. */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <Reviews
      title=""
      average={REVIEWS.average}
      count={REVIEWS.count}
      href={REVIEWS.profileUrl}
      quotes={quotes}
    />
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guarantee" level={4}>What guarantee comes with the work?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        The guarantee covers installation and workmanship for 2 years and the structural integrity of the joinery for 10 years. Hinges, runners and other hardware carry their manufacturer's warranty. If doors, drawers or other moving parts require adjustment during the 2-year aftercare period, we will return and adjust them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Questions" scribe>Asked before enquiring</SectionHead>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-12);max-width:var(--measure);border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)">
      <Faq q="How long do the first visit and the survey take?">
        The first visit usually takes 45 to 60 minutes, and the measured survey about an hour on site. Both run longer for a larger or more complex project. Before we arrive, you do not need to know every detail: asking the right questions is our job.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="Can new furniture match the woodwork already in the house?">
        Finishes are chosen to complement or closely match the furniture, joinery and flooring already in the room. Spray-painted, hand-painted, veneered and lacquered or oiled finishes are all possible, and the right one depends on the design and what it needs to match.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="How long does a job take?">
        Installation typically takes 2 to 3 days for a straightforward wardrobe wall, and closer to a week where on-site construction or hand-painting is involved. The lead time before work starts is typically 4 to 8 weeks. Where a project needs design, one week is set aside for it, with drawings issued for you to review by the end.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="What should I check before hiring a carpenter?">
        Ask for public liability insurance, a guarantee and an itemised fixed quotation before anything is booked. We carry five million pounds of public liability cover, and our guarantee is set out above.
      </Faq>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</AreaPage>
